    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.33003/japes.2026.v2i1.78-88",
    "abstract": "Understanding long-term hydro-climatic variability is vital for sustainable water resource management, especially in watersheds targeted for infrastructural development. This study examined the spatio-temporal trends and patterns of key hydro-climatic variables\u2014rainfall, relative humidity, temperature, and potential water deficit (PWD)\u2014in the Bagel Zungur Watershed, North-Central Nigeria, over a 40-year period (1981\u20132020). These parameters were selected due to the proposed dam project within the watershed. Monthly and annual climatic data were sourced from NASA POWER/CERES/MERRA-2 and Climate Engine datasets. Linear regression and time-series analyses were employed to assess long-term trends, variability, and deviations from mean conditions. The results indicate a general decline in rainfall of 10.44 mm (16.57%) accompanied by strong inter-annual variability range of 1,350.59 mm. Decadal pattern revealed steady decline from 1991-2000 (1177 mm), 2001-2010 (480.08 mm), and 2011-2020 (546.87 mm) with a longtime annual average of 888 mm respectively. Relative humidity also showed a decreasing trend (-0.13%) and a long-term average of 53%, while temperature exhibited a significant increase (0.04%) with annual mean temperature of 24\u00b0C, reflecting progressive warming across the watershed. Potential water deficit remained consistently high (-848.68), with peak deficits occurring between 2001 and 2010 (-1668.7), suggesting persistent hydrological stress despite a slight post-2011 improvement. Overall, the study provides a critical baseline for dam planning, water resource management, and climate adaptation in the watershed.",
    "field": "Hydro-climatic variability",
    "problem": "Planning for a proposed dam in the Bagel Zungur Watershed is challenged by the absence of a detailed, long-term analysis of hydro-climatic variability and potential water deficit, which is crucial for sustainable water resource management and ensuring the project's viability.",
    "method": "This study employed linear regression and time-series analyses on 40-year (1981\u20132020) satellite and climate datasets to assess long-term spatio-temporal trends of rainfall, relative humidity, temperature, and potential water deficit within the Bagel Zungur Watershed.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze the spatio-temporal trends of hydro-climatic variables and potential water deficit in Nigeria's Bagel Zungur Watershed from 1981-2020 to provide a critical baseline for sustainable water resource management and a proposed dam project.",
    "model": [
        "MERRA-2"
    ],
